</div>

<div class="stats">
  <div class="stat"><div class="n">505</div><div class="l">packets parsed</div></div>
  <div class="stat"><div class="n">48.6 KB</div><div class="l">payload bytes</div></div>
  <div class="stat"><div class="n">3</div><div class="l">hosts (2 in OT zones)</div></div>
  <div class="stat"><div class="n">5</div><div class="l">findings</div></div>
</div>
<div class="muted" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">Capture window: 2026-05-07 12:00:00 UTC → 2026-05-07 12:00:00 UTC</div>



<h2>Findings</h2>

  
  <div class="finding sev-critical">
    <h3><span class="badge sev-critical">critical</span>Telnet session observed (cleartext by definition)</h3>
    <div>1 Telnet packet(s) seen across 1 host(s). Credentials traversing these flows should be considered exposed.</div>
    
    <details>
      <summary>Evidence (1 sample)</summary>
      <pre class="evidence">10.10.0.20:23 (1 packet(s))
</pre>
    </details>
    
    <div style="margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Migrate the device(s) to SSH if supported, or place behind a jump host. Rotate any passwords used during the session window.</div>
    <div class="muted" style="margin-top:0.3rem;font-size:0.8rem;">id: <code>creds.telnet</code></div>
  </div>
  
  <div class="finding sev-critical">
    <h3><span class="badge sev-critical">critical</span>Internet-bound traffic from OT subnets</h3>
    <div>Saw 1 distinct flow(s) (600 bytes total) from hosts inside the configured OT subnets to public internet addresses. Plant networks should be egress-restricted at the IT/OT boundary.</div>
    
    <details>
      <summary>Evidence (1 sample)</summary>
      <pre class="evidence">10.10.0.5 -&gt; 8.8.8.8:80 (tcp, 5 pkts, 600 bytes)
</pre>
    </details>
    
    <div style="margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Audit the IT/OT firewall ruleset. OT zones should only egress to specifically allowed update servers and vendor cloud endpoints, brokered through a DMZ with logging. Block, then add explicit allow rules.</div>
    <div class="muted" style="margin-top:0.3rem;font-size:0.8rem;">id: <code>egress.ot_to_internet</code></div>
  </div>
  
  <div class="finding sev-high">
    <h3><span class="badge sev-high">high</span>EtherNet/IP CIP engineering-class services observed</h3>
    <div>1 CIP engineering-class request(s) (Set/Reset/Start/Stop/Forward Open with config) seen across 1 pair(s). These services change controller configuration or run state.</div>
    
    <details>
      <summary>Evidence (1 sample)</summary>
      <pre class="evidence">10.10.0.5 -&gt; 10.10.0.20 : SendRRData / Stop
</pre>
    </details>
    
    <div style="margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Limit which engineering workstations can talk to controllers on tcp/44818 + udp/2222. Lock controller keyswitches to RUN/REMOTE-ONLY where possible to refuse program downloads.</div>
    <div class="muted" style="margin-top:0.3rem;font-size:0.8rem;">id: <code>ics.cip_engineering</code></div>
  </div>
  
  <div class="finding sev-high">
    <h3><span class="badge sev-high">high</span>Modbus engineering-class commands on the wire</h3>
    <div>1 write/diagnostic Modbus call(s) observed across 1 client→server pair(s). Modbus has no authentication; any host that can reach a controller on tcp/502 can change plant state.</div>
    
    <details>
      <summary>Evidence (1 sample)</summary>
      <pre class="evidence">10.10.0.5 -&gt; 10.10.0.20 : fc=0x05 (Write Single Coil)
</pre>
    </details>
    
    <div style="margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Enumerate which hosts are allowed to write to controllers and ACL the rest at the switch/firewall. Consider Modbus-aware filtering (deep-packet inspection) in front of safety-critical PLCs.</div>
    <div class="muted" style="margin-top:0.3rem;font-size:0.8rem;">id: <code>ics.modbus_writes</code></div>
  </div>
  
  <div class="finding sev-high">
    <h3><span class="badge sev-high">high</span>S7Comm engineering-class commands on the wire</h3>
    <div>1 S7 engineering call(s) — 1 download op(s) across 1 client→server pair(s). S7Comm has no authentication; any host that can reach a controller on tcp/102 can read/write variables, download programs, or stop the CPU.</div>
    
    <details>
      <summary>Evidence (1 sample)</summary>
      <pre class="evidence">10.10.0.5 -&gt; 10.10.0.20 : fc=0x1A (Request download)
</pre>
    </details>
    
    <div style="margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Limit which engineering workstations can talk to controllers on tcp/102. For S7-1500 / TIA Portal environments, set the controller access level to &quot;no access (complete protection)&quot; or &quot;read access&quot; and require known-fingerprint TLS via Secure Communication. For older S7-300/400, use switch ACLs and physically lock the keyswitch to RUN.</div>
    <div class="muted" style="margin-top:0.3rem;font-size:0.8rem;">id: <code>ics.s7_engineering</code></div>
  </div>
  


